{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01BbFNyw2vR2dj1MYcPMh8fS","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"liquid lithium self-diffusion coefficient experimental NMR Murday Cotts Arrhenius\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"The dynamics of liquid 1-ethyl-3-methylimidazolium acetate measured with implanted-ion $^8$Li $\\\\beta$-NMR\",\"url\":\"https://arxiv.org/pdf/1907.03684\"},{\"title\":\"self diffusion coefficient study of liquid lithium\",\"url\":\"https://scholar.xjtu.edu.cn/en/publications/self-diffusion-coefficient-study-of-liquid-lithium/\"},{\"title\":\"link.springer.com\",\"url\":\"https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/BF00883553\"},{\"title\":\"s00231 011 0874 9\",\"url\":\"https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s00231-011-0874-9\"},{\"title\":\"link.aps.org\",\"url\":\"https://link.aps.org/doi/10.1103/PhysRevB.83.094302\"},{\"title\":\"Self-diffusion coefficients of ionic liquids measured by multinuclear benchtop NMR\",\"url\":\"https://magritek.com/?p=21016\"},{\"title\":\"li self diffusion in garnet type lisub7sublasub3subzr sub2subosub\",\"url\":\"https://research-portal.st-andrews.ac.uk/en/publications/li-self-diffusion-in-garnet-type-lisub7sublasub3subzr-sub2subosub/\"},{\"title\":\"subject:Self diffusion coefficients\",\"url\":\"https://fitforthem.unipa.it/subject:Self-diffusion coefficients\"},{\"title\":\"10.1088%2F0953 8984%2F6%2F26%2F003\",\"url\":\"https://doi.org/10.1088%2F0953-8984%2F6%2F26%2F003\"},{\"title\":\"Chinese Chemical Letters  2017, Vol. 28 Issue (2): 362-366   PDF\",\"url\":\"https://html.rhhz.net/zghxkb/20170237.htm\"}]\n\nThe search didn't return the Murday and Cotts paper itself or its Arrhenius parameters, so I can only point to where it is cited and what related work says.\n\n- **Murday & Cotts (1968):** Later papers cite their work, \"Self-diffusion coefficient of liquid lithium,\" in J Chem Phys 48:4938–4945 Murday JS, Cotts RM (1968) Self-diffusion coefficient of liquid lithium. J Chem Phys 48:4938–4945.\n- **Agreement with later simulation:** A 2012 study by Wang and Ni used an EAM potential to compute lithium's self-diffusion, and it reported that calculated self-diffusion coefficient is in good agreement with Murday's experiment results in atmosphere. It also derived an Arrhenius fit from its simulations (We get the Arrhenius equation according to the simulation results.). I did not retrieve that fit's numbers.\n- **Later PFG-NMR measurements:** A paper on liquid lithium isotopes used pulsed-field-gradient NMR between 454 K and 580 K. It reported a melting-point ratio of 6Li to 7Li diffusivities of 1.25, which it notes is significantly greater than the square root of the inverse mass ratio.\n- **Solid-lithium context:** A separate solid-lithium study fits self-diffusion with an Arrhenius-type expression, giving an activation energy of 12.0 kcal/mol for the leading term, but this concerns the solid phase, not the liquid.\n\nFor the exact Murday–Cotts Arrhenius coefficients, the 1968 paper itself or the Wang and Ni (2012) paper in *Heat and Mass Transfer* would be the next places to check.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"60e5bae0-74f3-4dab-b4d3-7504b260a251","uuid":"b6d69411-b611-4e70-afd5-fa3a1af4c0b5","timestamp":"2026-11-01T02:55:54.545Z","tool_use_result":{"query":"liquid lithium self-diffusion coefficient experimental NMR Murday Cotts Arrhenius","results":[{"tool_use_id":"srvtoolu_01Vz1WzaAvecPaT1vmfMASvf","content":[{"title":"The dynamics of liquid 1-ethyl-3-methylimidazolium acetate measured with implanted-ion $^8$Li $\\beta$-NMR","url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/1907.03684"},{"title":"self diffusion coefficient study of liquid lithium","url":"https://scholar.xjtu.edu.cn/en/publications/self-diffusion-coefficient-study-of-liquid-lithium/"},{"title":"link.springer.com","url":"https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/BF00883553"},{"title":"s00231 011 0874 9","url":"https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s00231-011-0874-9"},{"title":"link.aps.org","url":"https://link.aps.org/doi/10.1103/PhysRevB.83.094302"},{"title":"Self-diffusion coefficients of ionic liquids measured by multinuclear benchtop NMR","url":"https://magritek.com/?p=21016"},{"title":"li self diffusion in garnet type lisub7sublasub3subzr sub2subosub","url":"https://research-portal.st-andrews.ac.uk/en/publications/li-self-diffusion-in-garnet-type-lisub7sublasub3subzr-sub2subosub/"},{"title":"subject:Self diffusion coefficients","url":"https://fitforthem.unipa.it/subject:Self-diffusion coefficients"},{"title":"10.1088%2F0953 8984%2F6%2F26%2F003","url":"https://doi.org/10.1088%2F0953-8984%2F6%2F26%2F003"},{"title":"Chinese Chemical Letters  2017, Vol. 28 Issue (2): 362-366   PDF","url":"https://html.rhhz.net/zghxkb/20170237.htm"}]},"The search didn't return the Murday and Cotts paper itself or its Arrhenius parameters, so I can only point to where it is cited and what related work says.\n\n- **Murday & Cotts (1968):** Later papers cite their work, \"Self-diffusion coefficient of liquid lithium,\" in J Chem Phys 48:4938–4945 Murday JS, Cotts RM (1968) Self-diffusion coefficient of liquid lithium. J Chem Phys 48:4938–4945.\n- **Agreement with later simulation:** A 2012 study by Wang and Ni used an EAM potential to compute lithium's self-diffusion, and it reported that calculated self-diffusion coefficient is in good agreement with Murday's experiment results in atmosphere. It also derived an Arrhenius fit from its simulations (We get the Arrhenius equation according to the simulation results.). I did not retrieve that fit's numbers.\n- **Later PFG-NMR measurements:** A paper on liquid lithium isotopes used pulsed-field-gradient NMR between 454 K and 580 K. It reported a melting-point ratio of 6Li to 7Li diffusivities of 1.25, which it notes is significantly greater than the square root of the inverse mass ratio.\n- **Solid-lithium context:** A separate solid-lithium study fits self-diffusion with an Arrhenius-type expression, giving an activation energy of 12.0 kcal/mol for the leading term, but this concerns the solid phase, not the liquid.\n\nFor the exact Murday–Cotts Arrhenius coefficients, the 1968 paper itself or the Wang and Ni (2012) paper in *Heat and Mass Transfer* would be the next places to check."],"durationSeconds":5.546130545,"searchCount":1},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01BbFNyw2vR2dj1MYcPMh8fS","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01Nzp3XUua3PNsBJN5LZzfmG","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"liquid lithium density temperature correlation g/cm3 Davison handbook\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"ntrs.nasa.gov\",\"url\":\"https://ntrs.nasa.gov/api/citations/19680018893/downloads/19680018893.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"ntrs.nasa.gov\",\"url\":\"https://ntrs.nasa.gov/citations/19680018893\"},{\"title\":\"ntrs.nasa.gov\",\"url\":\"https://ntrs.nasa.gov/archive/nasa/casi.ntrs.nasa.gov/19680018893.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"LITHIUM (Li)\",\"url\":\"https://qedfusion.org/LIB/PROPS/PANOS/li.html\"},{\"title\":\"Operational conditions:\",\"url\":\"https://www.chemeo.com/mid/22-277-a/Choline-chloride_Urea_lithium-chloride\"},{\"title\":\"Lithium chloride · LiCl\",\"url\":\"https://processconvert.pages.dev/substances/lithium-chloride\"},{\"title\":\"core.ac.uk\",\"url\":\"https://core.ac.uk/download/132578668.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"core.ac.uk\",\"url\":\"https://core.ac.uk/works/150069412\"},{\"title\":\"- Publication Title:\",\"url\":\"https://mocat.library.unt.edu/catalog/885-14468\"},{\"title\":\"elogim.com\",\"url\":\"https://springerlink.bibliotecabuap.elogim.com/content/pdf/10.1361/105497198770341761.pdf\"}]\n\nI found the Davison report but not its density equation. The search returned the NASA report's abstract and a secondary page that gives a density fit, which I could not verify against the original.\n\n- **Davison source:** Compilation of thermophysical properties of liquid lithium [with bibliographies]; by Harry W. Davison. It is NASA TN D-4650, dated July 1968. The NASA full-text PDF (ntrs.nasa.gov, ID 19680018893) is the place to get the actual correlation. Its abstract says the properties were correlated as a function of temperature, using both experimental data and theoretical analyses, with extrapolation to about 1600 K. The snippets did not include the density equation itself.\n- **Secondary density fit (unverified):** A QEDfusion lithium property page gives a density correlation in g/cm³: rho = 0.5082 - 1.0336x10-4 T* - 4.8279x10-10 T*2 - 5.2853x10-12 T*3 (1) with T* = (T - 271.7). The page does not clearly attribute this equation to Davison, and it does not state the temperature unit. The offset 271.7 does not obviously match the stated melting point (180.5 °C, about 453.7 K), so treat the equation as unconfirmed until checked against TN D-4650.\n\nI'd check the NASA PDF for the density equation and its units, then compare it with the QEDfusion fit.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"60e5bae0-74f3-4dab-b4d3-7504b260a251","uuid":"81988464-180e-4f4e-82f5-f79ace95d5de","timestamp":"2026-11-01T02:55:56.138Z","tool_use_result":{"query":"liquid lithium density temperature correlation g/cm3 Davison handbook","results":[{"tool_use_id":"srvtoolu_01UeL59pKhyM4ayz1WTAr6xE","content":[{"title":"ntrs.nasa.gov","url":"https://ntrs.nasa.gov/api/citations/19680018893/downloads/19680018893.pdf"},{"title":"ntrs.nasa.gov","url":"https://ntrs.nasa.gov/citations/19680018893"},{"title":"ntrs.nasa.gov","url":"https://ntrs.nasa.gov/archive/nasa/casi.ntrs.nasa.gov/19680018893.pdf"},{"title":"LITHIUM (Li)","url":"https://qedfusion.org/LIB/PROPS/PANOS/li.html"},{"title":"Operational conditions:","url":"https://www.chemeo.com/mid/22-277-a/Choline-chloride_Urea_lithium-chloride"},{"title":"Lithium chloride · LiCl","url":"https://processconvert.pages.dev/substances/lithium-chloride"},{"title":"core.ac.uk","url":"https://core.ac.uk/download/132578668.pdf"},{"title":"core.ac.uk","url":"https://core.ac.uk/works/150069412"},{"title":"- Publication Title:","url":"https://mocat.library.unt.edu/catalog/885-14468"},{"title":"elogim.com","url":"https://springerlink.bibliotecabuap.elogim.com/content/pdf/10.1361/105497198770341761.pdf"}]},"I found the Davison report but not its density equation. The search returned the NASA report's abstract and a secondary page that gives a density fit, which I could not verify against the original.\n\n- **Davison source:** Compilation of thermophysical properties of liquid lithium [with bibliographies]; by Harry W. Davison. It is NASA TN D-4650, dated July 1968. The NASA full-text PDF (ntrs.nasa.gov, ID 19680018893) is the place to get the actual correlation. Its abstract says the properties were correlated as a function of temperature, using both experimental data and theoretical analyses, with extrapolation to about 1600 K. The snippets did not include the density equation itself.\n- **Secondary density fit (unverified):** A QEDfusion lithium property page gives a density correlation in g/cm³: rho = 0.5082 - 1.0336x10-4 T* - 4.8279x10-10 T*2 - 5.2853x10-12 T*3 (1) with T* = (T - 271.7). The page does not clearly attribute this equation to Davison, and it does not state the temperature unit. The offset 271.7 does not obviously match the stated melting point (180.5 °C, about 453.7 K), so treat the equation as unconfirmed until checked against TN D-4650.\n\nI'd check the NASA PDF for the density equation and its units, then compare it with the QEDfusion fit."],"durationSeconds":6.500559395,"searchCount":1},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01Nzp3XUua3PNsBJN5LZzfmG","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
